This release is meant to fix multiple security issues that are present=0Ain=
 the GRUB version we use (2.06+).=0A=0AUsers having replaced the GNU Boot p=
icture / logo with untrusted=0Apictures could have been affected if the pic=
tures they used were=0Aspecially crafted to exploit a vulnerability in GRUB=
 and take full=0Acontrol of the computer. In general it's a good idea to av=
oid using=0Auntrusted pictures in GRUB or other boot software to limit such=
 risks=0Abecause software can have bugs (a similar issue also happened in a=
=0Afree software UEFI implementation).=0A=0AUsers having implemented variou=
s user-respecting flavor(s) of=0Asecure-boot, either by using GPG signature=
s and/or by using a GRUB=0Apassword combined with full disk encryption are =
also affected as these=0Asecurity vulnerabilities could enable people to by=
pass secure-boot=0Aschemes.=0A=0AIn addition there are also security vulner=
abilities in file systems,=0Awhich also enable execution of code. When boot=
ing, GRUB has to load=0Afiles (like the Linux or linux-libre kernel) that a=
re executed=0Aanyway. But in some cases, it could still affect users.=0A=0A=
This could happen when trying to boot from an USB key, and also having=0Aan=
other USB key that has a file system that was crafted to take=0Acontrol of =
the computer.=0A=0AAt the time, no known exploits are known by the GNU Boot=
 maintainers.=0A=0AWhy it took so long.=0A--------------------=0A=0AThe 18 =
February, the GRUB maintainer posted some patches on the=0Agrub-devel maili=
ng list in order to notify people that there were some=0Asecurity vulnerabi=
lities in GRUB that were fixed, and which commit=0Afixed them.=0A=0AOne of =
the GNU Boot maintainers saw these patches but didn't read the=0Amails and =
assumed that a new GRUB release was near and decided to wait=0Afor it as th=
is would make things easier as GRUB releases are tested in=0Amany different=
 situations.=0A=0AHowever the thread posting these patches also mentioned t=
hat a new=0Arelease would take too much time and that the GRUB contributors=
 and/or=0Amaintainers already had a lot to deal with.=0A=0AIt took a while =
to realize the issue: a second GNU Boot maintainer saw=0Athe GRUB security =
vulnerabilities later on, and at this point they=0Arealized that nothing ha=
d happened yet on GRUB side yet and they=0Alooked into the issue.=0A=0AIn a=
ddition the computer of one of the GNU Boot maintainer broke,=0Awhich also =
delayed the review of the GNU Boot patches meant to fix=0Athis security iss=
ues.=0A=0AThese patches also contain fixes for the GNU Boot build system as=
 well=0Ato ensure users building GNU Boot themselves really do get an updat=
ed=0AGRUB version.=0A=0AAs this is a new release candidate, we also need he=
lp for reporting on=0Awhich computers and/or configuration it works or does=
n't work,=0Aespecially because we had to update to an unreleased GRUB versi=
on to=0Aget the fixes (see below for more details).=0A=0AOther affected dis=
tributions?=0A-----------------------------=0A=0AWe started telling the Can=
oeboot and Libreboot maintainer about the=0Aissue to later find out that th=
e issue was fixed since the 18=0AFebruary, and their users were also notifi=
ed via a news, so everything=0Ais good on that side.=0A=0AFor most 100% fre=
e distributions, using GRUB from git would be=0Aa significant effort in tes=
ting and/or in packaging.=0A=0AWe notified Trisquel, Parabola and Guix and =
the ones who responded are=0Anot comfortable with updating GRUB to a not-ye=
t released git=0Arevision. Though in the case of Parabola nothing prevent a=
dding a new=0Agrub-git package that has no known vulnerabilities in additio=
n to the=0Aexisting grub package, so patches for that are welcome.=0A=0AAs =
for the other distributions, most of them do support secure boot=0A(by supp=
orting UEFI secure boot), but they are probably aware of the=0Aissue as (ma=
intainers of) distributions like Debian or Arch Linux=0Aeither responded to=
 the thread on the GRUB mailing list, or were=0Amentioned as having fixed t=
he issue in that thread.=0A=0AAt the time of writing, the affected GRUBs ve=
rsions seems not to be=0Ablacklisted yet by UEFIs, so it also leaves some t=
ime to fix the=0Aissue, and things like GRUB password can usually be bypass=
ed easily=0Aunless people use distributions like GNU Boot or Canoeboot and=
=0Aconfigure both the hardware and the software to support a secure boot=0A=
scheme that respect users freedoms.=0A=0AAs for PureOS we just notified the=
m in a bug report as they have UEFI=0Asecure boot, but in another hand we d=
on't know if they are aware of=0Athat (they are based on Debian so it could=
 be inherited from Debian=0Aand not something supported/advertised), and be=
cause Purism (the=0Acompany behind PureOS) ships computers with their own s=
ecure boot=0Ascheme (PureBoot), since it works in a very different way we a=
re not=0Asure if people could be affected or not.=0A=0AReferences and detai=
ls=0A----------------------=0A=0AThis release should fix the following CVEs=
 affecting previous GRUB=0A2.06: CVE-2025-0690, CVE-2025-0622, CVE-2024-457=
75, CVE-2024-45777,=0ACVE-2024-45778, CVE-2024-45779, CVE-2024-45781, CVE-2=
024-45782,=0ACVE-2024-45783, CVE-2025-0624, CVE-2025-0677, CVE-2025-0684,=
=0ACVE-2025-0685, CVE-2025-0686, CVE-2025-0689, CVE-2025-1125.=0A=0AMore de=
tails are available in the "[SECURITY PATCH 00/73] GRUB2=0Avulnerabilities =
- 2025/02/18" thread From Daniel Kiper (Tuesday, 18=0AFebruary 2025, archiv=
ed online at=0Ahttps://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/grub-devel/2025-02/msg000=
24.html).=0A=0A=0A_______________________________________________=0AMessage=
 sent via Savannah=0Ahttps://savannah.gnu.org/=0A
